{% comment %}
  Shared cross-tier framing for the architecture chain (resp, comparch,
  subcomparch, impl) and the three supporting tiers (ref, vocab, policy) —
  siege's `_shared.py` pattern, moved into the bundle as a Liquid partial
  (v5-design-decisions.md §6). One source for what every generation and
  review prompt in this bundle needs to say about who reads its output,
  so a tightening here reaches every tier at once instead of drifting
  seven separate copies out of sync.

  Meaning-engine discipline (v5 §B.2.5, platform_content.md): name your
  downstream reader, and push against category-speak. "Handles user
  data" is not an answer to what this component does; the next tier
  reads this document to make decisions it cannot make for itself, and
  a vague sentence here becomes an invented assumption there — never a
  question the pipeline asks back. If you don't know something because
  the intake raft doesn't say, write down the gap as an explicit open
  question in your draft rather than filling it with plausible-sounding
  filler; a reviewer can act on a named gap and cannot act on a
  well-disguised guess.
{% endcomment %}
You are drafting **{{ tier_label }}** for the project described below.
Read every upstream document fully before writing; do not paraphrase
what you have not read.

**Who reads this next:** {{ downstream_reader }}. Write for that reader
specifically — not for a human skimming, not for a future you. If a
sentence would mean the same thing to any project, it is not doing its
job here; say the thing that is true of *this* project and would be
false of a plausible neighbor.

**Closed vocabulary, not closed thinking.** The grammar below is exact
and every element it allows is a decision, not a formality — an empty
or boilerplate element is worse than a shorter document that is honest
about what is still open. Where you are extending, hedging, or
narrowing a decision made upstream, say so and say why; silent
disagreement with an approved document is a defect the review pass
exists to catch, and it can only catch what you wrote down.
